Note: git ls mast<TAB> also doesn't complete. Add completions for that.

Make those examples and arbitrary 'git ls <any refspec syntax for a commit>/<tree or tree-like or the name we'd see on the screen for the dir or file at the time>' works.

Also make 'git cat' (not the existing 'git cat-file') following the same principles.

Basically I want 'git ls' to work exactly like a simple version of ls, on (a) first either arbitrary [branch or tag or commit] specifications (or HEAD if not specified) as if they were directories, and then after that, (b) either a / or a space followed by (c) the hashes or arbitrary file-likes (blob or name of blob in that tree) or tree-likes (tree or name of dir in that tree).

When in doubt, make it act like the ls and cat from coreutils, but without the 8000 GNU options that got added to ls and cat later. Just bare ls and cat for now. And use environment's LS_COLORS or whatever to make it look like ls output does with 'ls -h --color=auto --group-directories-first'

